    "finalResponse": "Earth has seasons primarily because of its **axial tilt**, not because of its distance from the Sun.\n\nThe Earth is tilted on its axis by about **23.5 degrees** relative to its orbital plane. As Earth orbits the Sun, this tilt remains fixed in direction relative to the stars, meaning different hemispheres lean toward or away from the Sun at different times of the year.\n\n- When a hemisphere tilts **toward** the Sun, sunlight hits that region more directly (higher **sunlight angle**), days are longer, and temperatures rise — creating **summer**.\n- When it tilts **away**, sunlight strikes at a lower angle, days are shorter, and temperatures drop — creating **winter**.\n\nThis variation in **sunlight angle** and day length is what drives the seasons.\n\nIn contrast, Earth’s distance from the Sun does vary slightly over the course of its elliptical orbit — but this has **very little effect** on seasons. In fact, Earth is closest to the Sun (perihelion) in early January, during Northern Hemisphere winter, and farthest (aphelion) in early July, during Northern Hemisphere summer. So distance isn't the main driver.\n\n**Source:**  \n[https://www.nasa.gov/learn/earth/earth-s-tilt-and-the-seasons](https://www.nasa.gov/learn/earth/earth-s-tilt-and-the-seasons)",
